  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on deviations from an aspiration level (2400) and prefers the lottery with a higher probability of exceeding this level. However, both lotteries have a 25% chance of exceeding 2400 (A: 4000; B: 6000), so the reason would not discriminate between them. The individual, in contrast, bases their choice on the number of winning events (i.e., count of non-zero outcomes), not on whether outcomes exceed a computed average. The aspiration level (2400) is not mentioned or implied, nor is any calculation of differences from a mean outcome. The individual’s reasoning is about quantity of gains, not their relation to a reference value derived from all outcomes. There is no evidence that the aspiration level was computed or used. The information considered (number of winning events) and the justification (more chances to win, even if probabilities are the same) are unrelated to the core mechanism of the decision reason (comparing outcomes to a mean and evaluating probability of positive deviations). Therefore, the decision reason was not used.",
